# Ownership boundaries, enforced by GitHub. Last matching pattern wins.
#
# Three roles, individuals rather than teams: one owner for the orchestrator
# runtime, one for the authoring platform, one or two covering all the modules.
# Teams can come later; a team with one person in it is a team for no reason.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠ BEFORE ENABLING — VERIFY EVERY HANDLE RESOLVES.
#
# GitHub SILENTLY IGNORES a pattern whose owner does not resolve. It does not
# warn, it does not fail: the line simply stops requiring a review. So a typo
# here does not read as an error, it reads as "anyone may merge this" — on
# whichever path you typed it against.
#
# Check them at Settings → Code owners after the first push. That page lists
# every unresolvable pattern. Do not turn on "Require review from Code Owners"
# until it is empty.
#
# @SuhasGangireddy is known good — it is already in use on this repository.
# The others below are inferred from git author names and have NOT been
# confirmed as GitHub logins.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


